The adequacy check confirms the claims do execute the normalized parse of the submitted `solution.mpy`, and their result cells are exact, not free. The failure is semantic rather than substitution: a satisfying universal input such as `"Åå"` has claimed/K result `2`, while both Python implementations return `1`; `"😀"` isolates a second defect, where the K model counts `4` rather than one Python character. I’m recording both as ground, machine-checked false-conclusion witnesses while completing the exhaustive rule inventory.
